  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 123 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nAs stated in the short term portion, temperatures remain below\nnormal in the long term portion of the forecast. The reasoning for\nthe cooler temperatures this week can likely be tied to El Nino.\nEl Nino seasons favor a split jet pattern with the northern jet\nbeing stronger and keeping the colder Canadian air away giving us\nwarmer temperatures. However this year the southern stream is\nstronger and so while we are not seeing any stronger cold surges,\na lack of a strong ridge will keep cooler air in place. It is\ninteresting when we talk about \"cooler\" air because this week we\nare mainly talking about high temperatures. We are over a week\npast our average first freeze showing the cooler air we have seen\nthis year has mainly been affecting highs, usually due to cloud\ncover blocking the sun during the day and insulating at night. \n\nA reinforcing surge of cold air arrives Tuesday keeping surface\nhigh pressure in place. Some models are showing a low chance\nshowers with this front but most areas will be dry and anyone\nreceiving rainfall will get very little. Thanksgiving will be dry\nthough chilly for anyone having outdoor plans and the next system\nto keep an eye on arrives Friday into Saturday. Models are\nconflicting on the timing and strength of an upper low diving down\ninto northern Mexico which could bring our next decent rain\nchances.\n\nHennig",
